course: dictionary
lesson: 1694
title: Chuckle and friends
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A chuckle is a quiet laugh. You might laugh with your mouth closed because something made you smile inside. Look at the clues around the word. «Grandpa chuckled: "Well, it worked on your daddy."» He is not roaring with laughter. He is pleased and gently amused.
explain: Chuckle is a laughing word, and there are many others: giggle, chortle, snigger, cackle, guffaw. Each one is a different kind of laugh. To choose the right one, ask how loud the laugh is and how the person feels. «It seems alive, chattering, chuckling, sparkling» tells you the river is making soft, happy sounds, not a loud noise.
explain: Try swapping your meaning into the sentence. If Grandpa chuckled means he laughed quietly, does the sentence still make sense? Yes, it does, so you have found the right meaning.
fact: «Chuckle» means to laugh quietly, often with your mouth closed, because something is amusing.
fact: As a noun, «a chuckle» means a soft, quiet laugh, as in «have a chuckle».
fact: A chuckle is quieter than a laugh. A guffaw is a big, loud laugh.
fact: Chuckle is a verb and a noun: «chuckles», «chuckled» and «chuckling» are all verb forms.
fact: In stories, «chuckled» often shows a kind grandparent or a sly character who is amused, not angry.
exercise: «Grandpa chuckled: "Well, it worked on your daddy."» What does chuckled mean? | *Laughed quietly, with a soft laugh | Fell asleep | Shouted angrily | Cried loudly
exercise: Look at «chattering, chuckling, sparkling». Which word is a laughing word? | *chuckling | river | sparkling | chattering
exercise: «The Gryphon chuckles: it's all her fancy.» What does chuckles tell you about the Gryphon? | He is crying | He is furious and shouting | *He is gently amused, not roaring with laughter | He is fast asleep
test: Leo says a chuckle is a loud roar of laughter. Is he right? | No, it is a kind of sneeze | Yes, it is very loud | Yes, but only at night | *No, a chuckle is a soft, quiet laugh
test: Which sentence uses chuckle as a noun, meaning a soft laugh? | Grandpa chuckles every day. | Grandpa will chuckle later. | *Grandpa gave a happy chuckle. | Grandpa was chuckling all day.
exam: Grandma gave a soft ___ at the joke, not a roar. | Stamp | Sob | *chuckle | Scream
